      "message": {
        "role": "tool",
        "tool_call_id": "call_sq4jKfL3BFfyi6gr6mQHOgRo",
        "content": "=== OBJECTIVE ===\n# Objective\n\nDescend as deep into the dungeon as you can and survive; ultimately win the game (retrieve the Amulet of Yendor and escape).\n\nSuccess: ascended\n\nDrive the game only through the `nethack` MCP tools.\n\n\n=== dungeons-of-doom.md ===\n# dungeons of doom\n\nThe Dungeons of Doom is the name given to the first half of the Mazes of Menace . They are 25 to 29 levels deep and contain a number of special levels and the entrances to several branches as well as the majority of special rooms and random dungeon features . The player starts on the first floor of the Dungeons of Doom and has to work their way down, eventually passing below the Castle into Gehennom . After retrieving the Amulet of Yendor from Gehennom, they must then return to the first floor, and climb the stairs to enter the Planes .\n\n\n=== stairs.md ===\n# stairs\n\nStairs in NetHack come in two glyphs: > for stairs down and < for stairs up. To descend to the next dungeon level, stand ON a > tile and call descend. Critical: < (stairs up) do NOT take you deeper. The goal is to descend through > tiles.\n\nIf descend fails ('you cannot go down here'), you are not on a > tile. Check UNDER PLAYER in the observation — it says 'stairs DOWN' or 'stairs UP' or the tile you are actually on. The @ glyph hides the tile beneath it.\n\nTo find stairs: scan VISIBLE FEATURES (lists every stairs/altar/fountain with (x,y) coords). Call move_to(x, y) to walk to a > tile, then descend.\n\n\n=== exploration.md ===\n# exploration\n\nExploration rewards methodical work. The map starts unrevealed; only tiles in sight are visible. Walking through corridors reveals adjacent tiles. autoexplore walks toward the nearest frontier (a known tile adjacent to an unseen tile).\n\nRecommended workflow on a new dlvl:\n1. autoexplore repeatedly until VISIBLE FEATURES shows stairs DOWN.\n2. move_to(x, y) using the listed > coordinates.\n3. descend.\n\nIf autoexplore reports 'level appears fully explored' but no > is visible, the level has a hidden passage. Walk to a dead-end and call search(times=10). When the harness emits an [autoexplore-loop: N short trips] hint, switch to search or move_to.\n\n\n=== hunger.md ===\n# hunger\n\nOn NetHackWiki, hunger can refer to any of the following:\n\nA ring of hunger\nThe voracious hunger property granted from eating or wearing a ring of hunger\nNutrition levels that signal that a hero is hungry (i.e. 50-149)\nThe \"ring hunger\" from wearing almost any type of ring\nThe similar nutrition drain from wearing an amulet...\n\n\n=== armor.md ===\n# armor\n\nArmor is a type of item that appears in NetHack. The hero and monsters can wear armor to improve their armor class. There are 7 slots for armor: helm, cloak, suit of body armor, shirt, shield, gloves, and boots.\n\n\n== Generation ==\nMost randomly generated armor is created with the following odds of each beatitude: 12.31% cursed, 78.96% uncursed and 8.72% blessed. The helm of opposite alignment, gauntlets of fumbling, fumble boots, and levitation boots all instead generate as 90.45% cursed, 8.6% uncursed, and 0.95% blessed—these items will be negatively enchanted 9⁄10 of the time, and are positively enchanted 1⁄100 of the time.\nBelow is a table describing the total probability of beatitude and enchantment:\n\nGiven the beatitude status, the chance of each enchantment is:\n\n\n== Description ==\nWearing a piece of armor lowers the wearer's armor class by a value equal to that armor's base AC. The armor's enchantment can increase or decrease the amount of AC gained from wearing it, and can increase or decrease the wearer's own AC accordingly. Erosion can lower the amount of base AC granted by worn armor, with a reduction of one point for each stage of erosion to a minimum of zero. Some types of armor...\n\n\n=== weapon.md ===\n# weapon\n\nIn NetHack, a weapon is an item used to attack opponents.\n\n\n== Description ==\nMost weapons are wielded with w and used to hit enemies; you cannot wield a weapon if you are handless or tiny. To attack with a wielded weapon, simply try to move into a monster's space, or use Shift + F to attack in any direction.\nSome weapons are missiles that are designed to be thrown or shot with t (or f to fire quivered weapons), and others require a launcher to be wielded in order to shoot them as ammo—still other items are not designed as weaponry, but can be improvised to serve as one, such as a cream pie. Hitting monsters properly with actual weapons can increase your skill in that weapon. \nYou can ready a second weapon for use, and use x to exchange between your primary and secondary weapons. Some roles can wield both weapons at once using the #twoweapon command. Pressing w, then - will unwield your current weapon and have you fight empty-handed; this is the default for Monks, who excel in fighting without a weapon, and Tourists that start the game without a tin opener.\n\n\n=== Weapon generation ===\nRandomly generated weapons have the following total probability of beatitude and enchantment:\nGiven the BUC...\n\n",
