ected to the rest of the dungeon?\n\nLooking at y7:\n```\ny7:          ######        ### r#########:####   #  ##     |....|###########\n```\nx23-25: `###`, x26: space, x27: `r`, x28-36: `#########`.\n\nSo at y7, the corridor `###` at x23-25 connects to the monster `r` at x27, which connects to `#########` at x28-36. The space at x26 separates x23-25 from x27. But `r` is at x27 - is the monster ON a corridor tile? In NetHack, monsters can be on any walkable tile. So x27 at y7 might be a corridor tile with a monster on it.\n\nBut the space at x26 (y7) means there's solid stone between the `###` at x23-25 and the corridor at x27. They're not connected!\n\nUnless... the reveal output is showing spaces for areas I haven't explored yet, and these are actually corridors. Let me think about this differently.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that the `reveal` function reveals the actual dungeon map, including areas I haven't explored. Spaces in the map represent \"solid stone\" (areas that are not part of the dungeon). Corridors are `#`, rooms are `.`, walls are `-` or `|`.\n\nSo the dungeon IS disconnected at these points. The corridor at x23-25 (y7) is NOT connected to the corridor at x27 (y7). They're separate pieces of corridor separated by solid stone.\n\nBut this can't be right for NetHack - the dungeon should be fully connected. Unless there are hidden passages that I need to search for.\n\nOh! That's it. The wiki page \"hidden-passage.md\" mentions hidden passages. In NetHack, there are hidden doors and corridors that don't show up on the map until you search for them. The spaces in the reveal output might be hiding secret doors!\n\nLet me check the hidden-passage wiki.",
